---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
---

<Layout 
  title="Why Does My Bra Band Ride Up My Back? Causes & Fixes | BraSizeChecker"
  description="Learn why your bra band rides up your back and how to fix it. Discover why loose bands force straps to carry support and how sister sizes solve band sliding."
  canonical="https://brasizechecker.com/fit-guide/band-rides-up"
>
  <Header />
  <main class="flex-1 w-full max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8 sm:py-12 md:py-16">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[
      { label: 'Fit Guide', url: '/fit-guide' },
      { label: 'Band Rides Up' }
    ]} />

    <header class="mb-10 text-center sm:text-left">
      <span class="inline-block py-1 px-3 rounded-full bg-canvas-soft-2 border border-hairline text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-primary mb-3">
        Fit Diagnostic
      </span>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-ink tracking-tight mb-4">
        Why Does My Bra Band Ride Up My Back?
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
        If the back of your bra band arches upward toward your shoulder blades throughout the day instead of sitting level across your ribcage, your bra is not providing proper support.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="space-y-10 text-body leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base">
      <!-- Quick Answer -->
      <section class="bg-canvas-soft-2 border-2 border-primary/30 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-2">Quick Answer</h2>
        <p class="text-body leading-relaxed font-medium mb-4">
          A bra band rides up because <strong>the band size is too large</strong> or the elastic has stretched out over time. When the band is too loose, it cannot anchor against your ribcage, forcing your shoulder straps to pull the back of the bra upward.
        </p>
        <div class="max-w-md mx-auto my-4">
          <img 
            src="/img/fit-band-rides-up.svg" 
            alt="Vector diagram demonstrating bra band riding up back toward shoulder blades due to loose underbust band" 
            width="400" 
            height="240" 
            class="w-full h-auto rounded-xl border border-hairline" 
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <span class="text-center text-xs text-mute mt-2 block font-medium">Figure 1: Loose bra band arching upward toward shoulder blades</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Possible Causes -->
      <section class="bg-surface border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">Plausible Causes</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="p-4 bg-canvas-soft-2/60 border border-hairline rounded-xl">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-ink mb-1 text-sm">1. Using the Traditional "+4" Sizing Method</h3>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-body">Many older bra fitting charts add 4 inches to your underbust measurement, resulting in a band that is too large to anchor securely around your ribcage.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 bg-canvas-soft-2/60 border border-hairline rounded-xl">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-ink mb-1 text-sm">2. Worn-Out Band Elasticity</h3>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-body">Over months of wear and washing, elastane and spandex fibers degrade, loosening the band beyond its original structural capacity.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 bg-canvas-soft-2/60 border border-hairline rounded-xl">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-ink mb-1 text-sm">3. Over-Tightened Shoulder Straps</h3>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-body">Attempting to lift your bust by cranking down shoulder straps pulls a loose back band upward toward your neck.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- What to Check -->
      <section class="bg-surface border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">Practical Fit Checks</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <li><strong>Mirror Check:</strong> Stand sideways in front of a mirror. The band should form a completely horizontal straight line around your torso.</li>
          <li><strong>Two-Finger Rule:</strong> You should be able to slide no more than two fingers beneath the band at your back.</li>
          <li><strong>Hook Fastener Check:</strong> A new bra should fit snugly on the outermost hook, allowing you to tighten it as elastic relaxes over time.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- What Size Change Might Help -->
      <section class="bg-surface border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">Recommended Size Adjustment</h2>
        <p class="mb-4">
          To fix a riding-up band, move down one band size. However, to maintain the exact same cup volume capacity, you must simultaneously move up one cup letter letter in your new smaller band size:
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 bg-canvas-soft-2 border border-hairline rounded-xl text-center mb-6">
          <span class="text-xs font-bold text-primary uppercase tracking-wider block mb-1">SISTER SIZE EXAMPLE</span>
          <p class="text-lg font-bold text-ink">If your 36C band rides up &rarr; Try a 34D sister size</p>
        </div>
        <p>
          Calculate your exact smaller-band sister size using our <a href="/sister-size-calculator" class="text-primary font-semibold hover:underline">Sister Size Calculator</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Calculator CTA -->
      <section class="bg-primary text-white rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 text-center sm:text-left flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-between gap-6 shadow-md">
        <div>
          <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-1">Re-Calculate Your Baseline Size</h2>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-canvas-soft-2">Use our direct underbust measurement tool to verify your correct band size.</p>
        </div>
        <a href="/#calculator" class="flex-shrink-0 h-12 px-6 bg-surface hover:bg-canvas-soft-2 text-primary font-bold text-sm rounded-xl shadow-xs transition-colors flex items-center gap-2">
          <span>Calculate My Size &rarr;</span>
        </a>
      </section>

      <!-- Related Fit Guides -->
      <section class="bg-surface border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">Related Fit Guides</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs sm:text-sm font-semibold">
          <a href="/fit-guide/straps-falling-down" class="p-3 bg-canvas-soft-2 rounded-xl hover:text-primary transition-colors">&bull; Why Do My Bra Straps Keep Falling Down?</a>
          <a href="/fit-guide/band-too-tight" class="p-3 bg-canvas-soft-2 rounded-xl hover:text-primary transition-colors">&bull; Why Does My Bra Band Feel Too Tight?</a>
          <a href="/fit-guide/bra-cup-gaping" class="p-3 bg-canvas-soft-2 rounded-xl hover:text-primary transition-colors">&bull; Why Do My Bra Cups Gap?</a>
          <a href="/fit-guide" class="p-3 bg-canvas-soft-2 rounded-xl hover:text-primary transition-colors">&bull; Return to Fit Guide Hub</a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
